\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

We evaluate SICA against Self-Consistency (SC) across seven models from four base architectures (instruction-tuned, reasoning-specialized, and distilled variants) on four reasoning domains, with more than 2,100 total evaluation instances.

\paragraph{Models.} Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, and Qwen3-14B (non-thinking mode)~\citep{yang2024qwen25}, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3~\citep{jiang2023mistral}, LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{dubey2024llama3}, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B~\citep{guo2025deepseekr1}, and QwQ-32B~\citep{qwen2024qwq}. Parameter counts range from 7B to 32B.

\paragraph{Datasets.} FOLIO~\citep{han2022folio} ($n=204$) provides formal logic problems with gold FOL annotations for the oracle control (\S\ref{sec:oracle}). ProofWriter~\citep{tafjord2021proofwriter} tests multi-step deductive reasoning ($n=600$). LogiQA~\citep{liu2020logiqa} evaluates logical reasoning ($n=200$). StrategyQA~\citep{geva2021strategyqa} covers commonsense reasoning ($n=200$ for LLaMA-3.1-8B; $n=60$ for Qwen2.5-14B). GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} ($n=30$) and AIME 2024 ($n=22$) serve as operating-regime probes.

\paragraph{Parameters.} $K=12$ traces at $T=0.7$. Extraction at $T_{\text{ext}}=0.3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}). Z3~\citep{demoura2008z3} timeout: 10 seconds.

\paragraph{Metrics.} Accuracy (\%), $C_0$ (\S\ref{sec:c0}), and $\Delta = \text{SICA} - \text{SC}$ in percentage points (pp). McNemar exact test at $\alpha = 0.05$; 95\% bootstrap CIs (10{,}000 resamples) for $n \geq 200$.


\subsection{Main Results: A Regime-Dependent Effect}
\label{sec:main-results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} reports results across all conditions sorted by SC accuracy. SICA effectiveness depends on the model's baseline accuracy regime.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{SICA vs.\ SC across all conditions, sorted by SC accuracy. $\Delta$ = SICA $-$ SC (pp). $\dagger$: $n \leq 60$ (suggestive). $\ddagger$: $n = 30$ pilot. $^a$McNemar exact test. \textbf{Bold}: $p < 0.05$.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrrl@{}}
\toprule
Model & Domain & $n$ & SC (\%) & SICA (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) & $p^a$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Low-accuracy regime (SC $\leq$ 55\%)}} \\
Qwen2.5-14B & StrategyQA$^\dagger$ & 60 & 53.33 & 56.67 & $+$3.33 & 0.50 \\
Mistral-7B & FOLIO & 204 & 54.41 & \textbf{59.31} & $\mathbf{+4.90}$ & \textbf{0.013} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{High-accuracy regime (SC $\geq$ 63\%)}} \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & FOLIO & 204 & 63.24 & 60.78 & $-$2.45 & 0.267 \\
DeepSeek-R1-7B & FOLIO+PW$^\dagger$ & 60 & 71.67 & 68.33 & $-$3.33 & 0.480 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & StrategyQA & 200 & 72.00 & 74.00 & $+$2.00 & 0.424 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO+PW & 804 & 76.24 & 76.49 & $+$0.25 & n.s. \\
Qwen2.5-7B & FOLIO & 203 & 77.34 & 76.85 & $-$0.49 & 1.000 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & LogiQA & 200 & 78.50 & 78.00 & $-$0.50 & 1.000 \\
Qwen3-14B & FOLIO & 204 & 83.82 & 84.31 & $+$0.49 & 0.655 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Degenerate ($C_0 < 5\%$)}} \\
QwQ-32B & AIME$^\dagger$ & 22 & 72.70 & 72.70 & 0.00 & --- \\
Qwen2.5-14B & GSM8K$^\ddagger$ & 30 & 96.67 & 96.67 & 0.00 & --- \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\textit{Oracle upper bound (gold FOL/DSL + Z3)}} \\
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO & 204 & 75.49 & \textbf{89.71} & $+$14.22 & $<$0.001 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & ProofWriter & 600 & 75.17 & \textbf{86.67} & $+$11.50 & $<$0.001 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

In the low-accuracy regime (SC $\leq$ 55\%), SICA provides significant gains. Mistral-7B on FOLIO achieves $\Delta = +4.90$\,pp ($p = 0.013$, $b = 12$, $c = 2$, $n = 204$): 12 problems flip from incorrect to correct while only 2 flip in the opposite direction. Qwen2.5-14B on StrategyQA shows a directionally consistent $+3.33$\,pp ($n = 60$, suggestive).

Above approximately 60\% SC, no condition reaches significance. Seven conditions (SC $\geq$ 63\%, combined $N = 1{,}875$) produce $|\Delta| \leq 3.33$\,pp, all nonsignificant. Qwen2.5-14B across three powered datasets (FOLIO+PW $n = 804$, LogiQA $n = 200$) yields $|\Delta| \leq 0.50$\,pp. Qwen3-14B (SC $= 83.82\%$) shows substantial generational improvement over Qwen2.5-14B on the same benchmark, yet produces $\Delta = +0.49$\,pp ($p = 0.655$): generational capability gains lift both methods equally. Two degenerate conditions (QwQ-32B, GSM8K) have $C_0 < 5\%$, which reduces SICA to SC by construction.

The boundary is sharp: LLaMA-3.1-8B on FOLIO (SC $= 63.24\%$) already shows $\Delta = -2.45$\,pp. The transition occurs between 55\% and 63\% SC. Figure~\ref{fig:regime_scatter} visualizes this regime dependence. \S\ref{sec:entropy} provides mechanism evidence for this threshold. The oracle rows confirm that gold constraints yield $+14.22$\,pp on FOLIO and $+11.50$\,pp on ProofWriter (both $p < 0.001$), proving the aggregation mechanism is sound (\S\ref{sec:oracle}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{f2_regime_scatter.pdf}
\caption{SICA effectiveness by Self-Consistency accuracy across nine model-dataset conditions. Solid markers indicate $n \geq 100$; hollow markers indicate $n < 100$ (underpowered). In the favorable regime (SC $\leq$ 55\%, green shading), Mistral-7B achieves $+4.90$\,pp ($p = 0.013$). In the capability trap (SC $\geq$ 63\%, red shading), all seven conditions produce $|\Delta| \leq 3.33$\,pp, none significant.}
\label{fig:regime_scatter}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Oracle Control: The Aggregation Mechanism Is Sound}
\label{sec:oracle}

Oracle variants replace self-extracted constraints with gold annotations: FOL formulas from FOLIO ($n = 204$) and DSL logic programs from ProofWriter ($n = 600$). The oracle evaluates each gold constraint against each candidate answer via Z3 directly, isolating constraint quality from aggregation mechanics.

Oracle-SICA achieves 89.71\% on FOLIO versus 75.49\% for SC ($+14.22$\,pp, $p = 2.0 \times 10^{-7}$, $b = 29$, $c = 0$), with Z3 gold agreement of 95.1\% (194/204). On ProofWriter, Oracle-SICA reaches 86.67\% versus 75.17\% ($+11.50$\,pp, $p = 9.21 \times 10^{-15}$), with Z3 gold agreement of 97.1\% (577/594). Relational subtypes benefit most: RelNeg $+17.86$\,pp, RelNoneg $+13.01$\,pp (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:pw_oracle_subtype}).

The oracle gaps define recoverable potential. Even Mistral-7B's significant $+4.90$\,pp captures only 34.5\% of the FOLIO oracle ceiling ($+14.22$\,pp). Extraction quality remains the binding bottleneck across regimes.


\subsection{Entropy-Conditioned Analysis}
\label{sec:entropy}

The regime boundary in \S\ref{sec:main-results} could reflect model architecture rather than accuracy. We condition SICA effectiveness on per-problem answer entropy for two models with contrasting aggregate outcomes on FOLIO-204: Mistral-7B ($\Delta = +4.90$\,pp) and Qwen2.5-14B ($\Delta = -0.49$\,pp on the same benchmark).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{SICA $\Delta$ (pp) by answer entropy. The aggregate regime difference is driven by the proportion of high-entropy problems in each model's distribution. Qwen2.5-14B high-entropy bin ($n = 21$) reflects $\approx$1 problem difference.}
\label{tab:entropy}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{Mistral-7B} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen2.5-14B} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
Entropy & $n$ (\%) & $\Delta$ & $n$ (\%) & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
Low $[0, 0.5)$ & 65 (31.9) & 0.0 & 135 (66.2) & 0.0 \\
Med $[0.5, 1.0)$ & 66 (32.4) & $+$4.5 & 48 (23.5) & $-$4.2 \\
High $[1.0, \infty)$ & 73 (35.8) & $+$9.6 & 21 (10.3) & $+$4.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:entropy} and Figure~\ref{fig:entropy_delta} show that answer entropy mediates SICA effectiveness. For Mistral-7B, gains increase monotonically with entropy: $+9.6$\,pp on high-entropy problems ($n = 73$, approximately 7 problems flipped). The aggregate $+4.90$\,pp is driven by this subpopulation, which comprises 35.8\% of Mistral's distribution vs.\ only 10.3\% of Qwen's.\footnote{Qwen2.5-14B's high-entropy bin ($n = 21$, $+4.8$\,pp) is directionally consistent but reflects approximately one problem difference; the medium-entropy reversal ($-4.2$\,pp) further suggests bin-boundary sensitivity. Finer-grained analysis is warranted when additional high-entropy data becomes available.} Qwen concentrates 66.2\% of problems in the low-entropy bin where SICA has zero effect. Stronger models produce more consistent traces with lower entropy, which limits the subpopulation where constraints can provide value.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{f3_entropy_delta.pdf}
\caption{(a) Mean SICA $\Delta$ (pp) by answer entropy bin with 95\% bootstrap CIs. Mistral-7B gains increase monotonically with entropy; Qwen2.5-14B shows no reliable pattern (high-entropy bin $n = 21$, approximately one problem difference). (b) Entropy distribution: Mistral-7B allocates 36\% of problems to the high-entropy bin where SICA is most effective, vs.\ only 10\% for Qwen2.5-14B.}
\label{fig:entropy_delta}
\end{figure*}

Mechanism metrics confirm the causal chain. Unanimous answer rate: 18.6\% (Mistral) vs.\ 58.8\% (Qwen). When SICA overrides SC, the override is correct 70.6\% of the time for Mistral but only 28.6\% for Qwen. Confirmation bias (fraction of problems where $>$80\% of constraints agree with the majority answer): 31.9\% (Mistral) vs.\ 62.3\% (Qwen). MAX-SAT satisfiability ratio: 0.961 (Mistral) vs.\ 0.991 (Qwen), evidence that Mistral's constraints contain more genuine tension for the solver to exploit. These metrics trace a causal path: high model accuracy $\rightarrow$ consistent traces $\rightarrow$ low entropy $\rightarrow$ high confirmation bias $\rightarrow$ non-discriminative constraints $\rightarrow$ MAX-SAT degenerates to counting.


\subsection{Constraint Ablation}
\label{sec:ablation}

If high-accuracy constraints are non-discriminative, MAX-SAT should produce the same predictions as simpler rules. On Qwen2.5-14B (FOLIO + ProofWriter, $n = 804$), MAX-SAT and count-only agree on 803 of 804 predictions; wins (16) and losses (14) approximately cancel for a net gain of $+$2 problems (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:ablation}). Self-extracted constraints carry no signal beyond vote counts in the high-accuracy regime.


\subsection{Remediation Strategies}
\label{sec:interventions}

Six strategies target distinct hypothesized causes of the capability trap across prompt, architecture, and solver dimensions (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:remediation}). None produces significant improvement. Contrastive prompting worsens discriminativeness (6.0\% vs.\ 8.2\% baseline). Cross-model extraction (Mistral-7B from Qwen2.5-14B traces) produces 0\% discriminative rate: trace structure determines constraint content regardless of extractor identity. Qwen3-14B lifts SC and SICA equally ($\Delta = +0.49$\,pp, $p = 0.655$). Solver-level interventions encounter structural barriers in each case: 0.9\% UNSAT trigger rate for Z3 feedback~\citep{kambhampati2024position}, reversion to SC at consistency filtering threshold $T \geq 4$, and 56.4\% of constraint pairs sharing zero Z3 variables across traces.

